sentences.t:
Grand alegría es entre todos essos cristianos
con mio Çid R[o]y Diaz, el que en buen ora nasco.
Yal creçe la barba e vale allongando;

sentences.l:
Great joy is among all those Christians
with my Çid Roy Díaz, he who in good hour was-born.
Now-to-him grows the beard and goes-for-him lengthening;

sentences.t:
Los que f[o]ron después todos son pagados;
veelo mio Çid que con los averes que avién tomados,
que sis pudiessen ir, fer lo ien de grado.

sentences.l:
Those who were after all are satisfied;
sees-it my Çid that with the goods that they-had taken,
that if-themselves they-could go, do it-they-would of will.

sentences.t:
“si vos quisiéredes, Minaya, quiero saber recabdo
de los que son aquí e comigo ganaron algo;
meterlos he en escripto, e todos sean contados,

sentences.l:
“if you should-wish, Minaya, I-want to-know account
of those who are here and with-me won something;
put-them I-shall in writing, and all let-them-be counted,

sentences.t:
Mandólos venir a la corth e a todos los juntar,
quando los falló, por cuenta fízolos nonbrar:
tres mill e seys çientos avie mio Çid el de Bivar;

sentences.l:
He-ordered-them to-come to the court and all them to-gather,
when them he-found, by count made-them to-name:
three thousand and six hundred had my Çid he of Bivar;

sentences.t:
Si a vos ploguiere, Minaya, e non vos caya en pesar,
enbiar vos quiero a Castiella, do avemos heredades,
al rey Alfonsso mio señor natural;

sentences.l:
If to you it-should-please, Minaya, and not to-you it-fall into grief,
send you I-want to Castiella, where we-have estates,
to-the king Alfonsso my lord natural;

then for me kiss-ye-him the hand and firmly him-it beg-ye
for my wife [doña Ximena] and my daughters [natural],
if should-be his grace that-me-them he-should-let to-take-out.
